well we can speak now to the former  conservative mp and education secretary  justin greening great to have you on the  programme uh today morning sophie  yesterday was pride and back in 2016 you  chose that day to tweet  today is a good day to say i'm in a  happy same-sex relationship i campaigned  for stronger in but sometimes you're  better off out i absolutely love  this photo of you i have to say  um how important was that moment for you  i mean it was it was life-changing in a  way um  i've been a cabinet minister for quite a  while  and  not being frank about my relationship  had re i genuinely felt it had held me  back i didn't feel like doing interviews  on you know my personal life as much as  i possibly would have done and i  realized that i had to become part of  the solution  and i couldn't think of a better time  to do that tweet than on the day of  london pride back in 2016 it was  actually as you alluded to just after  the the eu referendum and  really from that moment on i just think  for me it was like a weight off my  shoulders and  you know i do so much campaigning now on  leveling up and social mobility but i  think you know you can't be at your best  if you can't be yourself and so  for me life got a lot better when i i  could be myself  i find it  quite hard i guess in some ways to  really understand that you know you're  someone who is an incredibly successful  woman you're confident you've been a  cabinet minister you've done numerous  interviews including on this show for  many years  and yet you felt  that it it did take quite a long time  for you to in your words to be yourself  yeah i just think it's why things like  london pride and all those other  you know marches and celebrations um  around the country matter because  actually we're on a journey and and i'm  gonna guess  there may be some i was a cabinet  minister  watching other people like david mundell  say this is who i am  and so  yes at the end of the day  it is a very personal um thing to do but  i also think it was up to people like me  to show some leadership almost if you  weren't part of the solution then you  were part of the problem you were sort  of saying you thought there was a  problem with your relationship and i  really don't think there is  so i all i would say is it was a hugely  positive step for me there's a lot more  campaigning to be done on lgbt plus  rights on on that um what campaigning do  you think needs to be done what issues  well we've seen homophobic hate crime  rise actually quite significantly over  the past four years i think when you  look at access to healthcare  mental health issues  there's a whole range of  different aspects i think for the lgbt  plus community  that  people would like to see better  addressed but also i think when you look  at say for example women's rights over  in the states and the rolling back of  roe v ward  on abortion rights now discussions  around whether that will translate  across other rights  like for the lgbt plus community i think  just this underlying assumption in the  past maybe the rights generally get  better but that's not always the case so  we have come a long way we've now got  same-sex marriage you can have adoption  obviously as well all sorts of things  but actually we shouldn't just assume  that we won't get pushed back and i  think that's why pride yesterday and all  of the campaigning that people do  matters so much it's interesting about  that um you know kind of rights under  attack if you that feeling um are you  concerned about same-sex marriage in the  u.s  well only time will tell but it really  worries me  that you can have a country like the  states with its democracy  and then  judges who have been ultimately  political appointees almost randomly  over different presidential  terms depending on who died when with so  much power  and i think  then the challenge is yes of course  codify these rights in legislation but  if you also then have a  stateside politics and democracy that is  often gridlocked  spit half and half down the line between  republicans and democrats then it is  hard for them to actually do that so  you're just left with a supreme court  taking away rights but a political  system not able to put them in place for  the long term where they should be in  law on roe versus wade and um  are you concerned about the what it  means to the uk we've seen of course  some mps calling for the right to an  abortion to be included in the british  bill of rights  i think it puts a question out there  that none of us really  had anticipated which is that women's  rights  may be pushed back  when actually the debate is how do we  move them forward still so i hope that  within parliament surely there has to be  a consensus  that it's a question of how you move  forward not moving backwards but i think  what the states tells us is that we  can't just assume that and i think for  not just female parliamentarians but but  you know men and women the cross-party  consensus as well that we've had  i think it's time to redouble those  efforts whether it's on gender pay gap  and some of those policy areas that  government's got whether it's the  campaigning around um  around abortion whether it's around just  generally seeing women do better across  society all of those things really  really matter i think  um  at the beginning when i was talking to  you about you know different campaigns  for lgbt you mentioned homophobic hate  growing going up which is again  something that in the research the  interview  i saw and actually was quite sort of  shocked by i guess and it's not what i  had expected you kind of think that  these things will just progressively get  better  but why it may be an impossible question  but why do you think it does seem as  though these crimes are going up  i think  i think it's because  there  there are people in every country but  including ours who just think this has  all gone too far  and and in spite of all of the broader  progress  um across the mainstream in a sense of  society  there are some parts of  our society who just don't accept that  and the problem is i think particularly  also in an internet age they're able to  sort of find one another and have those  conversations and self-validate them  which is why it does really matter that  you keep the fight going on lg lgbt plus  rights  um  now that i have you i do need to ask you  obviously about the allegations in the  conservative party and we've seen of  course the resignation of chris pincher  um teresa's coffee said that the prime  minister wasn't aware of in her very  careful language specific allegations  when he was appointed to be  deputy chief whip in february um  is that good enough  well look i think any time you end up  with an mp embroiled in these sorts of  allegations one of two things is going  to happen either the mp themselves will  take responsibility  and own it and work out what they do  next or it ends up being the party led  by a prime minister so one of those two  things is going to happen either  chris pincher as we get to the bottom of  these allegations will have to take  responsibility and decide what he does  but if that is not done then yes it will  reflect on the wider conservative party  particularly in parliament  led by boris johnson and that's why i  think for a lot of the parliamentary  party this comes back to  boris johnson's leadership now the way  out of this in the long term setting  aside what needs to happen on this  particular case  is to do exactly what caroline knows  karen bradley to highly respect to  senior female parliamentarians have  suggested which is to get a code of  conduct in place so we are not seeing a  prime minister and a party having to  just decide on the hoof  in each specific case it is a clear  process that people go through and then  everybody is  transparent and clear cut about what  would happen next and it is now time to  put this on a much firmer footing you  say time to put it on a firmer footing  because as you say this is not really an  isolated case we've had five conceptual  mps resigning or being suspended in the  last year  for allegations of sexual uh misconduct  it's pretty extraordinary really is this  now having an impact on the effectively  the brand of the conservative party  i think in the end  it's all it's all about delivery boris  johnson has an agenda of leveling up  delivering equality of opportunity he  has to get on with that  but i think if people feel that delivery  isn't happening fast enough and then  they see these other aspects of how the  party is behaving in parliament and it  gives you this sense that actually  there's not a focus on that delivery for  for joe public like there needs to be  then it it creates this broader  impression of  a party that's in government but not  really driving this country forward  which is absolutely  what we need right now britain has had  long-term persistent systemic  inequality of opportunity  and it will take leadership drive energy  and policy  to change that  and that's why it's so important that  there are some clear decisions taken  about these sorts of cases so that the  government can get on  with that agenda for which i actually  think there is cross-party  support but at the moment  of course it feels like attention is  anywhere apart from that  um just finally uh going back to the  start of the interview um you obviously  came out of pride were you there  yesterday i wasn't able to be there  yesterday because i had a family  birthday so it was probably one of the  first years i've missed it since that  tweet but i'll look forward to being  there  uh next year  definitely very common  photos they were absolutely fantastic  thank you so much it's great to talk  justin greening there thank you

